android apk builder for primarily DoL, built with Flutter

This is the only Android shell in the repo. It replaced a Capacitor build that
lived at devTools/apkbuilder; that tree has been removed, and the top-level
MEMORY.md records what state each piece of it ended in.

# dependencies

-   **Flutter 3.47.7 stable** (Dart 3.13), with `flutter` on PATH.
-   **JDK 21.**
-   **Android SDK** with platform 36, build-tools 36.0.0 and NDK 28.2.13676358.
    This repo already has one at `../androidsdk`.

Gradle is not needed: Flutter ships a wrapper in `android/`.

The NDK looks unnecessary -- nothing here compiles native code, and
`android/app/build.gradle.kts` deliberately does not set `ndkVersion`. It is
still required: AGP reports a default `ndkVersion` whether or not you set one,
and the Flutter Gradle plugin then insists on installing it during
configuration.

# one-time setup

    ./setup_deps.sh

This runs `flutter pub get` and assembles `assets/web/` from the compiled game.

# building

    npm run apk:debug
    npm run apk:release

The apk lands in the repository's `dist/` folder. Debug builds get a `_debug`
suffix on the application id so they install alongside the official app instead
of replacing it. That also gives them a separate WebView data directory, so the
two installs do not share saves despite reporting the same origin.

Release builds need your own keystore:

    npm run keystore:adopt     copy the key the Capacitor build already uses
    npm run keystore:new       generate a brand new one

**Use `keystore:adopt` if you have ever shipped a Capacitor build.** Android
refuses an update signed by a different key, so a fresh key would leave every
existing install stuck on the old app. `key.properties` and the `.jks` are
gitignored: back both up somewhere that is NOT this repo, because losing the key
means you can never publish an update to an app already on a device.

Before building, make sure `game/01-config/sugarcubeConfig.js` is configured for
a mobile build (`enableLinkNumberify: false`). See "Changing the build version
and type" in the top-level README. That toggle is per-build and should not be
committed.

# apk size

A release build is **arm64-v8a only**: `scripts/build_app_flutter.js` passes
`--target-platform=android-arm64`, because Flutter's default of arm64-v8a plus
armeabi-v7a plus x86_64 ships the engine's native libraries and a Dart snapshot
per ABI, which is most of the difference from the 26.4 MB Capacitor apk. The
game payload itself — 50.5 MB of HTML plus 13.3 MB of images — is byte-identical
to what the Capacitor build shipped.

The trade is deliberate: no 32-bit-only phones, and no x86 emulator. Google Play
requires 64-bit support, so publishing is unaffected. To test on an x86
emulator, add `android-x64` next to `android-arm64` in that script rather than
dropping the flag — armeabi-v7a is the one with no remaining use.

# do not change the storage origin

Saves live in `localStorage`, which is partitioned by origin. That origin is
`kStorageOrigin` in `lib/src/storage_origin.dart` -- `https://localhost` -- and
it is also half of the on-disk identity together with the `applicationId` in
`android/app/build.gradle.kts`. Changing either silently gives every existing
player an empty save.

An origin is scheme + host + **port**, so `http://localhost`,
`https://localhost:8080` and `https://appassets.androidplatform.net` are all
*different* origins from `https://localhost`, and every one of them would wipe
the saves. That rules out the usual ways of serving a local web app. Instead
`GameWebView.shouldInterceptRequest` answers `https://localhost/*` straight out
of the asset bundle, the same trick Capacitor's `WebViewLocalServer` used.

Those values are pinned in `storage-origin.json`, and
`scripts/verify_storage_origin.js` fails the build if they drift, if the
applicationId drifts, or if anything calls `WebView.setDataDirectorySuffix`. To
invalidate existing saves deliberately, re-run with `DOL_ALLOW_ORIGIN_CHANGE=1`
and update the lock file in the same commit.

    npm run verify:origin

# exporting saves

`www/flutter_additions.js` defines the `saveAs()` global that the game's export
code calls. It rides over a JavaScript handler into `MainActivity.kt`, which
opens the system save dialog (the Storage Access Framework) -- the same
`ACTION_CREATE_DOCUMENT` picker the Cordova and Capacitor builds used. There is
no first-party Flutter plugin that opens the *system* picker, which is why the
Kotlin is still here.

# the splash

One splash, and it is the system's: `LaunchTheme` points
`windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` at the app mark and nothing in Flutter draws
over it. The Flutter overlay that used to be held over the story load is gone,
along with the MutationObserver and the CSS rule hiding SugarCube's loading
screen. Android 12+ always draws its own splash icon, so having one in Flutter as
well meant the mark appeared twice, at two different sizes, and holding the second
one meant seconds of dead time before the player saw anything.

SugarCube's own loading screen now covers the story load rather than a blank dark
screen. Stock it is a bare grey circle: a 100px ring with a 24px `#7f7f7f` border,
two seconds per rotation, not even centred, with the "Loading…" text hidden by
`text-indent:9999em`. `www/flutter_additions.js` restyles it into a thin line
that sweeps left to right, echoing the `#ui-bar` that slides in from the left,
with the label restored. It is monochrome on purpose: the game's UI is greyscale
(`#111` page, `#222` surfaces, `#444` borders, `#eee` text).

Two traps when editing that CSS: do not target `#init-screen>div`, because
SugarCube hides `#init-no-js` and `#init-lacking` that way and unhiding them puts
"JavaScript must be enabled" beside the loader; and the line and label cannot use
flex `gap`, because the line is made from absolutely positioned pseudo-elements
which are out of flow and ignore it.

`NormalTheme`'s window background is flat `#111111` rather than the marked
drawable so no second mark flashes between the system splash and Flutter's first
frame. `transparentBackground: true` on the WebView keeps that handover from
flashing white.

To see the loading screen without building an APK, run `.preview/build-preview.js`.

# notches and system bars

The app is edge to edge (`SystemUiMode.edgeToEdge` plus
`android:windowLayoutInDisplayCutoutMode="shortEdges"`), which is what gives the
story the whole screen instead of a box inset by the system bars. Content is
pushed back out of the cutouts by `SafeArea` in `lib/src/app.dart`.

Do not use `env(safe-area-inset-*)` in the page for this: Chromium derives those
insets from the display cutout, so on a three-button-navigation device they
resolve to 0 and the story ends up under the navigation buttons.